Pipes: give non-blocking writers a 64 KiB capacity

Pipes on both file systems queued every write without limit: PIPEFS in
JavaScript memory, WasmFS on the wasm heap, until the heap ran out. A
writer that fills a pipe until write() fails with EAGAIN, or until
poll() stops reporting POLLOUT, never stopped.

Hold non-blocking writers to Linux's default capacity, 64 KiB, with
PIPE_BUF's rule that a write of up to 4096 bytes goes in whole or not at
all: a write that doesn't fit fails with EAGAIN, and a larger one is cut
short. A blocking writer can't wait for a reader, so it still queues
everything. poll() reports the write end writable only while a PIPE_BUF
write fits, and a PIPEFS read wakes a writer waiting in poll(). WasmFS's
DataFile gets a getWriteRoom() hook, which pipes implement, and the
syscall layer, which has the open file's flags, applies it.

Upstream: https://github.com/emscripten-core/emscripten/issues/27831 (open)
Draft: docs/upstream/emscripten-pipe-capacity.md

